<!-- Quote — the quote layout (vertically centered) in flow mode, and the deck's only **inverted
     slide**. `<slide invert>` swaps bg ↔ fg and surface ↔ muted on this slide only — the source's
     `color="fg"` stays as is, giving a dark slide in light themes and a light slide in dark themes.
     In PPTX it is a color-map override, so the theme colors still drive the slide.

     One big quotation mark carries the weight of the slide. The emphasis is text color, not `<mark>`:
     the highlighter color is chosen by the theme for a light background, so on an inverted slide a
     light highlight would sit under light text. -->
<slide id="quote" layout="quote" invert enter="rise">
  <text role="title" size="160" color="accent1" align="center">“</text>
  <blockquote size="50" color="fg" align="center">The gap between “a file that opens” and<br/>
  <span color="accent1">“a file you can edit”</span> is the product.</blockquote>
  <shape type="rect" fill="accent1" border="none" w="72" h="4" self="center"/>
  <text role="body" color="muted" align="center">— from the testing guide, on why a person still opens the PPTX by hand</text>
  <note>So every time something changes, we open it in PowerPoint or Keynote ourselves.</note>
</slide>
